PART 5 — ANIMATION LIFECYCLE AND SIMULATION TIMING

15. The original animation must complete naturally

Study these existing pieces carefully:

* CombatState.update(dt)
* CombatState.endAttack()
* CombatState.interruptAttack()
* Fighter.update(dt, ctx)
* Fighter.startAttack()
* Fighter.cutAttack()
* Fighter.updateState()
* SpriteAnimator.play()
* SpriteAnimator.update()

A critical implementation detail is that CombatState.update(dt) advances attack time and may end an attack before the rest of Fighter.update() processes the current frame’s combat requests.

Do not ignore this ordering.

A repeated press occurring on the same simulation step in which the current attack naturally completes must be handled consistently.

Capture or resolve repeat intent while the originating attack is still identifiable.

Then dispatch the follow-up at the correct point in the update lifecycle.

Do not allow the current attack to disappear before the system can recognize the repeat press.

16. Distinguish natural completion from early termination

A queue should execute only when its originating attack genuinely finishes through its normal animation lifecycle.

It should not execute merely because combat.attack becomes null.

An attack can stop because of:

* Natural completion.
* Another attack canceling it.
* A jump.
* A Dash.
* A Shield or Deflect.
* A technique or summon.
* Being hit.
* Paralysis.
* Ground contact.
* A landing cancel.
* A special attack motion completing early.
* A bounce.
* A reset, respawn, or match transition.

Several of these paths use endAttack().

Therefore, an unconditional combat.attack === null check is insufficient.

Implement reliable lifecycle detection.

An explicit completion reason or tightly scoped natural-finish event may be appropriate if the existing architecture needs one.

Avoid adding a complicated event bus merely for a small local feature.

Required invariant: A queued follow-up is released only when its original attack ended by natural completion—not when some other action canceled or interrupted it.

Preserve existing exceptions for attack motions and landing-cancel mechanics.

Do not force an attack that intentionally ends upon contact to continue executing a fictional full-length animation.

17. No animation restarts

While the first attack remains active:

* Its attack record stays the same.
* Its animation progresses normally.
* Its time increases only through normal simulation.
* Its animation frame progression remains uninterrupted.
* Its active hitboxes remain governed by the original attack.
* Its projectile-release flags remain intact.
* Its hit-confirm and multi-hit state remain intact.

SpriteAnimator.play() should restart only when a genuinely new animation begins or another existing gameplay rule legitimately requires it.

Do not add a restart: true call whenever the player presses a repeated combat button.

The queued alternate is allowed to start at its own first frame after the previous animation has finished.

That is not restarting the previous animation.

18. Respect hitstop

Hitstop freezes attack animation progress.

A queued Combo Assist follow-up must not execute during hitstop.

If the player repeats the active attack during a valid hitstop interval, that press may register as the queued follow-up, provided the originating attack is still valid and the player has not been interrupted.

The follow-up waits until the original attack can finish.

Do not age a combo queue using wall-clock time.

Pausing the game must not make the queue expire.

Use deterministic simulation state.

19. Respect fixed-step determinism

All combat changes must remain compatible with CONFIG.sim.step, currently 1/60 second.

Do not use:

* setTimeout.
* setInterval.
* Wall-clock timestamps.
* Date.now().
* performance.now().
* Unseeded randomness.
* Animation-frame callbacks as gameplay timers.

Do not execute extra simulation steps to make a combo happen faster.

Equivalent input sequences must produce equivalent combat results regardless of the browser’s rendering frame rate.

