PART 8 — IMPROVING THE OVERALL FIGHTING FEEL

26. Prioritize responsiveness over unnecessary animation shortcuts

Alva’s attacks vary in speed because they have different frame counts, frame rates, startup, active phases, recovery, and special motion rules.

Those differences are part of the fighting system.

Do not flatten every attack into an identical animation duration.

A quick basic strike and a powerful finishing attack should not feel the same.

Instead, improve how player inputs transition between those existing moves.

The goal is:

The game should respect the player’s input while respecting the move’s intended commitment.

Good responsiveness does not mean every animation can be canceled for free.

Good responsiveness means the player can predict what will happen when pressing a button.

27. Preserve skill expression

Experienced players should still benefit from:

* Timing their attacks deliberately.
* Selecting different follow-up attacks.
* Knowing their character’s real combo routes.
* Managing cooldowns.
* Using directional movement.
* Controlling spacing.
* Performing Dash cancels.
* Using hit-confirm opportunities.
* Choosing when to defend.
* Using Combat Assist or disabling it.
* Controlling jumps, fast falls and aerial attacks.
* Managing Energy and Launch Point.

Combo Assist is an accessibility and usability improvement, not an automatic optimal-combo generator.

Do not introduce a system that chooses the highest-damage attack for the player.

Do not automatically chase opponents indefinitely.

Do not change the attack’s damage or launch strength based on whether Combo Assist was used.

Do not guarantee a true combo.

A mechanically executed follow-up can still miss, be blocked, or leave the opponent time to recover.

28. Avoid creating a button-mashing exploit

A beginner should be able to press Attack 1 repeatedly and see a sensible alternating sequence.

That does not mean each input should grant extra speed or damage.

Test sustained button mashing against:

* A stationary opponent.
* A shielding opponent.
* An airborne opponent.
* An opponent being launched away.
* A target near a ledge.
* A high-Launch-Point target.

Ensure that pressing repeatedly does not cause:

* More hitboxes per attack.
* Shorter cooldowns.
* Repeated projectile spawning.
* Skipped recovery.
* Faster attacks than the definitions allow.
* Guaranteed loops.
* Extra Energy.
* Disappearing commitment frames.

Mashing should become easier to understand, not become inherently superior to skillful play.

29. Review perceived dropped inputs

Once the queued follow-up is implemented, inspect the surrounding ordinary attack and cancellation pathways.

Look for places where a player presses a valid control, receives no obvious response, and cannot understand why.

Focus especially on:

